Exact MP3 reader (Linux and portable builds)
--------------------------------------------
Linux and `--features portable` builds decode MP3 input with
src/portable/audio/mp3/, a project-written Rust implementation (no `unsafe`
code, standard library only, integer arithmetic only) that needs no setting. It
is a compatibility implementation: its purpose is to produce what Apple's
AudioToolbox produces for an MP3 file, and its behavior was recovered from
Apple's own binaries by static analysis, instruction-level transcription and
recorded runtime states of the AudioCodecs component and AudioToolboxCore on
macOS 27.2 (build 26B123) on one reference machine. It therefore contains
numeric constants and algorithms that reproduce Apple's behavior, among them 33
constant tables that are bit-exact copies of data read from the arm64e slice of
AudioCodecs (SHA-256 70e5d4a56b24d037b940328916df496a20267eb206f8e316216ae7bef69a1626);
the table files (tables.rs, back_tables.rs) and the module header (mod.rs) name
the binary and the addresses each table was read from. No Apple binary and no
Apple source code is included, and the reader calls no Apple API. The scripts
that generated and verified the tables, the research corpora and the recorded
Apple results are not part of the public source; the checked-in test fixtures
under tests/fixtures/mp3-exact/ are generated audio with recorded results. This
is a statement of provenance, not a legal opinion, and no legal clearance of
the reader, its constants or its algorithms is claimed. The v0.3.0
source also compiled, in its unit tests only, a generated register-level model of
one Apple routine (the polyphase synthesis, synth_machine.rs and synth_ref.rs, with
the test that compared the reader's synthesis against it); those two files and that
test were removed in v0.4.0 and are kept only as private research material. The
reader's remaining tests replay recorded AudioToolbox results. The macOS default build uses AudioToolbox itself and does not
use this reader. An earlier open-source
MP3 decoder written from ISO/IEC 11172-3 and 13818-3 (shipped opt-in in v0.2.0,
with Huffman and window numbers transcribed from the LAME 3.100 archive) was
replaced by this reader and is no longer part of the source.

Exact AAC encoder and decoder (Linux and portable builds)
---------------------------------------------------------
Linux and `--features portable` builds write AAC output and read AAC input
with src/portable/aac/ (wired in by src/portable/audio.rs and
src/portable/audio/aac_read.rs), project-written Rust that uses only the
standard library and the project's own arithmetic modules and needs no
setting. The AAC modules contain no `unsafe` code apart from one unit-test
module, built only for aarch64 tests, that compares a kernel with the
hardware instructions it models (the encoder modules and the decoder's
container and bit-stream modules forbid `unsafe` code, and the rest of the
decoder denies it). The shared arithmetic modules they call (src/portable/arm.rs,
math.rs, dft.rs and vdsp.rs, of which arm.rs, dft.rs and vdsp.rs contain small
`unsafe` blocks) and the flush-to-zero control of src/fp_mode.rs (FPCR and
MXCSR access) are not AAC-specific. These modules are compatibility
implementations: their purpose is to produce what Apple's AudioToolbox
produces for the engine's call sequence (the encoder for the engine's one
configuration, the decoder for the reads the engine makes), and their
behavior was recovered from Apple's own binaries by static analysis,
instruction-level transcription and recorded runtime states of the
AudioCodecs component and AudioToolbox on macOS 27.2 (build 26B123) on one
reference machine. They therefore contain numeric constants and algorithms
that reproduce Apple's behavior, among them constant tables that are bit-exact
copies of data read from the arm64e slice of AudioCodecs (SHA-256
70e5d4a56b24d037b940328916df496a20267eb206f8e316216ae7bef69a1626). The AAC
modules hold 19 table and data files: 10 binary data files (*.f32) and 9 Rust
files of constant tables (tables.rs in six stage directories, tables_data.rs,
tables_gen.rs and gen_tables.rs). Most of their contents are such copies; the
exceptions are named in the file headers and comments: the scalefactor-band
offsets are the published ISO/IEC 14496-3 tables, the quantiser's scalefactor
code-length table is derived from the ISO code lengths, the entries of the
decoder's dequantisation table from 1024 up are correctly rounded powers
computed with a math library, and the binary data file
src/portable/aac/bits/data/powf43_8208.f32 (powf(i, 4/3) for i below 8,208, the
decoder's float pulse table, whose first 8,192 entries the encoder's quantiser
also uses and the project's own powf reproduces) holds values of a power
function. The table files and module
headers name the binary and the addresses each table was read from. No Apple
binary and no Apple source code is included, and the encoder and decoder call
no Apple API. The scripts that generated and verified the tables, the research
corpora and the Apple results beyond the fixtures are not part of the public
source; the recorder tools under tools/portable/aac/ call AudioToolbox on
macOS to record fixtures and are not used by the engine at run time, and the
checked-in test fixtures under tests/fixtures/aac-encode/,
tests/fixtures/aac-decode/ and tests/fixtures/aac-reader/ are generated audio
with recorded results. The project is not affiliated with Apple. This is a
statement of provenance, not a legal opinion, and no legal clearance of the
encoder, the decoder, their constants or their algorithms is claimed. AAC is a
patented format, and patent licensing may apply to encoders and decoders in
some jurisdictions; the project grants no patent rights and gives no legal
advice. The macOS default build uses AudioToolbox itself and does not use these
modules.

